<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Shamkhalate of Tarki</h1><p class="st-meta">1642 CE – 1867 CE · capital Tarki</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Kumyk state on Dagestan's Caspian coast, heir of the old shamkhals, which Peter the Great visited in 1722 and Russia absorbed in 1867.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1642, as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kazikumukh-shamkhalate">Kazikumukh Shamkhalate</a> split, the shamkhals moved their seat from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a> mountains to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarki">Tarki</a>, on the Caspian plain near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhachkala">Makhachkala</a>. The new state was centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumyks">Kumyk</a> people and controlled the coastal road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The shamkhals ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumyks">Kumyk</a> nobles and villages and claimed authority over much of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, receiving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> and courting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in turn. The plain provided grain, livestock and trade routes. During his Persian campaign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> was received at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarki">Tarki</a> in 1722 by Shamkhal Adil-Girey, who later rebelled and died in Russian exile.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The shamkhals accepted Russian protection in 1806 and held Russian military rank; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehdi-ii-of-tarki">Mehdi II</a> was a loyal ally against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam-shamil">Shamil</a>. In 1867 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> abolished the shamkhalate and incorporated it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan-oblast">Dagestan Oblast</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shamkhalate-of-tarki.jpg" alt="Shamkhalate of Tarki" width="250" height="175"><figcaption>Image: Kamil Aliev · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Shamkhalate_of_Tarki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1642 CE – 1867 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tarki</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adil-Girey, Mehdi II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kumyk</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tarki Shamkhalate, Tarkov Shamkhalate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1754&amp;focus=shamkhalate-of-tarki" aria-label="Shamkhalate of Tarki on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5827 652 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shamkhalate of Tarki lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6147 908L6144 933M6147 908L6281 1235M6147 908L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6144 933h0M6281 1235h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6147 908h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6147 908h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tarki. <a href="/atlas?year=1754&amp;focus=shamkhalate-of-tarki">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
